ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186145099 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "789c7308-d1a5-5b0c-9c15-595b95d781b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147162 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147162 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $4 \\%$ of $35?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147162 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "034e763d-a3e1-52e8-90a7-9fac9ce11083" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147162 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $35$, and the percentage is $4 \\%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n4 \\% = 4 \\div 100 = 0.04\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =4%\\times 35 \\\\  & =0.04\\times 35 \\\\  & =1.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4 \\%$ of $35$ is $1.4.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n4 \\% = \\dfrac{4}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =4%\\times 35 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{100}\\times 35 \\\\  & =\\frac{140}{100} \\\\  & =1.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4 \\%$ of $35$ is $1.4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147163 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147163 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $140 \\%$ of $30?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147163 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5fc07bc-6168-5525-acfb-a36ee021773d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147163 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $30$, and the percentage is $140\\%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n140 \\% = 140 \\div 100 = 1.4\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =140%\\times 30 \\\\  & =1.4\\times 30 \\\\  & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $140 \\%$ of $30$ is $42.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n140 \\% = \\dfrac{140}{100} = \\dfrac{140 \\div 20}{100\\div 20} = \\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =140%\\times 30 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\times 30 \\\\  & =7\\times \\frac{30}{5} \\\\  & =7\\times 6 \\\\  & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $140 \\%$ of $30$ is $42.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147164 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13110" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147164 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $3.6 \\%$ of $25?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8dde0f33-28c8-569b-a1f8-9390d8dafc35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147164 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $25$, and the percentage is $3.6 \\%.$ \n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n3.6 \\% = 3.6 \\div 100 = 0.036\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =3.6%\\times 25 \\\\  & =0.036\\times 25 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3.6 \\%$ of $25$ is $0.9.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n3.6 \\% = \\dfrac{3.6}{100} = \\dfrac{3.6 \\times 10}{100 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{36}{1,000}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =3.6%\\times 25 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{1,000}\\times 25 \\\\  & =\\frac{900}{1,000} \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3.6 \\%$ of $25$ is $0.9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218